  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $({x}^{2}-3x)+({x}^{3})$\n\nII. $(1-2x)-(2-{x}^{2})$\n\nIII. $(x+1)\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Expression II is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
