><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-141233-d\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">d</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">I and II only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-141233-e\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">e</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">III only</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"questionWidget-buttonBar\"><div class=\"questionWidget-submitButton disabledButton\" style=\"display: none;\">Submit</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-spinnerFrame\"><div class=\"questionWidget-spinner\" style=\"display: block;\"></div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-result\"><div class=\"questionWidget-correctIncorrectIcon\"><img src=\"/img/correct-answer.svg\"></div><div class=\"questionWidget-correctText\">Correct</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-explanationHeader\">EXPLANATION</div><div class=\"questionWidget-explanation\"><p>Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is <em>always</em> a polynomial. </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Polynomials are <em>not</em> closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is <em>not</em> always a polynomial. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Expression I is a polynomial. It's a sum of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under addition. </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Expression II is a polynomial. It's a difference between two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under subtraction.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Expression III gives a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\" </p>\n</div></div>",
